why so many people are developing monkey  pox around europe all at the same time  is perplexing scientists at its worst  the disease can look alarming but the  viral strain behind the current outbreak  can cause just a milder rash that  blisters over and scabs rather like  chickenpox the puzzle is why a disease  endemic to parts of africa and  transmitted largely by animals is  suddenly spreading so widely between  people most likely through direct skin  to skin contact  they're viruses that are new in human  populations whether or not they've been  there for several years and all of a  sudden got into an amplifying population  is not clear it may have been that they  were transmitting in a slow chain of  transmission  among family members or somebody related  and all of a sudden it got into a  population which was more promiscuous  had more physical contact and therefore  it amplified in its transmission the  health secretary tweeted that he has  updated g7 health ministers underlining  how seriously this is being taken the  outbreak is very different to covet  numbers are small but with another  animal virus spreading in humans this is  no time for complacency  the first case was isolated at a  specialist infectious diseases unit at  guy's and thomas's hospital in london on  may the 7th they had recently returned  from nigeria and it looked like a rare  travel related case but since then there  have been many more and there's no  obvious connection between them  most of the known cases are in london  with some confirmed elsewhere in the uk  but there are also parallel outbreaks in  spain and portugal and cases reported in  france italy germany sweden belgium the  us canada and australia and this is a  disease that typically doesn't spread  well between people  due to the reports of infections  worldwide and the quick information also  from the robert koch institute doctors  and patients in germany were isolated  based on the knowledge available so far  we assume that the virus is not easily  transmissible and that this outbreak can  be contained  this can succeed if we act very quickly  the uk government has ordered more doses  of smallpox vaccine which hasn't been  routinely used in the uk since 1971  it is also 85 effective against monkey  pox and it can be given after exposure  to the virus to stop symptoms that along  with public awareness and contact  tracing will be key to bringing the  outbreak under control  thomas more sky news
 

